macho_ptrslots_arm64, macho_ptrslots_offset_arm64 -- arm64 Mach-O pointer slots
held as LC_DYLD_CHAINED_FIXUPS chain entries.

What they are
  macho_ptrslots_arm64 (33,872 bytes, SHA-256
  c6ada51d489644071e0066eace19aef4d15492b3260e3bf3c70372970442ddf7) is built
  from macho_ptrslots.c: GH #1333's source plus one imported pointer.
    _audit_label_ptr  @ 0x100004000 -> _audit_label  0x100000440 (rebase)
    _audit_func_ptr   @ 0x100004008 -> _audit_target 0x1000003c8 (rebase)
    _audit_import_ptr @ 0x100004010 -> _audit_imported            (bind, ordinal 0)
  ld64.lld writes DYLD_CHAINED_PTR_64 (format 2): the first word reads
  0x0010000100000440 on disk (the chain's `next` field in its high bits), the
  bind 0x8000000000000000.

  macho_ptrslots_offset_arm64 (SHA-256
  cd5265aed04ecf2f3d125782892ebdf6eb66ea80c3f783fb3f99fe3a74b6f642) is the same
  image in Apple ld64's layout, DYLD_CHAINED_PTR_64_OFFSET (format 6), whose
  rebase target is an image-base offset (0x0010000000000440). Generated by
  macho_ptrslots_offset_arm64.py, which rewrites only the format fields and the
  rebase words in place (its code signature is stale; nothing reads it).

How they were built (no macOS SDK; host clang + the rustup-bundled ld64.lld)
  clang -target arm64-apple-macos11 -O1 -g0 -fno-inline -fno-optimize-sibling-calls \
        -c macho_ptrslots.c -o p.o
  LLD=$(rustc --print sysroot)/lib/rustlib/$(rustc -vV | sed -n 's/host: //p')/bin/gcc-ld/ld64.lld
  "$LLD" -arch arm64 -platform_version macos 11.0 11.0 -fixup_chains \
         -undefined dynamic_lookup -e _main -o macho_ptrslots_arm64 p.o
  python3 macho_ptrslots_offset_arm64.py macho_ptrslots_arm64 macho_ptrslots_offset_arm64
  Ubuntu clang 14.0.0-1ubuntu1.1, LLD 20.1.8 (rustc 1.90.0). Prebuilt because CI
  has no Mach-O linker.

What they pin
  The loaded image reads the rebased pointers 0x100000440 / 0x1000003c8 and a
  zero import slot in both layouts; `--option dynrelocs off` reads the raw words.
